{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Target\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/executor.py` — **790 LOC** on origin/main against the 800-LOC hard cap (10 lines of headroom).\n\nTen lines of headroom.\n\n## How to do it\nRead the file and derive the seam from the CALL GRAPH — do not take any hint above as the answer.\nExtract a cluster of functions that already call each other and that share one concern. The commit\nmessage must NAME the concern boundary you chose and why the call graph supports it.\n\n## Constraints (inherited from the epic — all non-negotiable)\n- Split along LOGICAL CONCERNS / ARCHITECTURAL BOUNDARIES, never mechanically to hit a number.\n- NEVER raise the limit; `.github/module-size-limit.txt` is locked.\n- No resulting file under 100 LOC; target 200-500 LOC each.\n- Leave REAL headroom — landing at 798 recreates the problem (that is how Gerrit 1916 failed).\n- Behaviour-preserving: existing tests for this area must pass UNMODIFIED. If a test must change,\n  the split was not behaviour-preserving — stop and reconsider the seam.\n- Serena `find_referencing_symbols` for references, THEN `grep` for each moved symbol AS A STRING\n  (monkeypatch.setattr / getattr / importlib). Serena returns EMPTY, not an error, for `Any`\n  receivers, so the grep is mandatory.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/workflow/executor.py` is under the cap with real headroom, and every new file is >= 100 LOC.\n- [ ] The split follows an existing call-graph seam; the commit message names the concern boundary.\n- [ ] Behaviour unchanged: existing tests covering this area pass UNMODIFIED.\n- [ ] The CI module-size gate passes without touching `.github/module-size-limit.txt`.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; the complexity ratchet shows no new or increased entries.\n- [ ] All references updated — verified by BOTH a semantic reference search AND a string grep.\n\n## Chosen seam (derived from the call graph, 2026-08-19)\n\n**Concern boundary: the step-kind I/O CONTRACT MODEL (declaration + static compatibility) vs the\nRUN ENGINE (control flow, expression resolution, dispatch, recording).**\n\n`executor.py` today holds two clusters that share no call edges:\n\n1. *Contract model* - `StepContract`, `STEP_CONTRACTS`, `contract_for`, `input_schema_for`, and the\n   3-state shallow structural check (`shallow_contract_check` -> `_schema_is_opaque` /\n   `_field_is_opaque` / `_primitive_kinds`). Internal edges only: `input_schema_for` -> `contract_for`\n   -> `STEP_CONTRACTS`; `shallow_contract_check` -> its three private helpers. Its consumers are the\n   STATIC / edit-time surface: `editor.py`, `editor_contracts.py`, `lint_refs.py`, and the\n   interpreter's pre-dispatch input validation.\n2. *Run engine* - `new_run_id`, `_capture_nondeterminism`, `RunState`, `RunResult`, the expression\n   resolver, `static_order` / `_terminal_step` / `_guard_passes`, `run_workflow`, `_dispatch`,\n   `_step_record`, and the snapshot TTL sweep.\n\nThe run loop NEVER calls cluster 1. The only edge across the seam is `register_step` writing\n`STEP_CONTRACTS`, so cluster 1 moves to a new `step_contracts.py` and `executor.py` imports it -\none direction, no cycle. This is the same pattern the file's two prior splits used\n(`recorder.py`, `runners.py`): move the cluster, re-export the public names from `executor` so\nevery existing import point keeps working.\n\n### Why the OTHER candidate clusters must NOT move\n- **Expression resolution** (`resolve_value` / `_resolve_one`) carries the ONE `# read-via:` marked\n  line budgeted to `llm/workflow/executor.py` in `tests/scripts/test_llm_seam_clean_heldout.py`\n  (`_MARKER_BUDGET`, exact count 1). Moving it turns that budget RED.\n- **`run_workflow`** is a live `monkeypatch.setattr` target (`test_code_review_gate_session.py`,\n  `test_code_review_provenance.py`, `test_plan_review_cancel.py`), so it stays put.\n- **`StepContext` / `StepResult` / `STEP_REGISTRY`** are read as MODULE ATTRIBUTES\n  (`_ex.StepContext`, `_ex.STEP_REGISTRY`) across ~50 test modules; they stay, so nothing that binds\n  to the module object moves.\n- The Burr tripwire (`tests/unit/workflow/test_executor_tripwire.py`) scans `executor.py` +\n  `interpreter.py` for banned scheduler/retry imports and for the trigger-list prose. The moved\n  cluster contains NO control flow and NO third-party imports, and the docstring stays, so the\n  tripwire keeps exactly its intended scope and needs no change.\n- `.github/complexity-baseline.json` has ZERO entries keyed to `llm/workflow/executor.py`, so no\n  baselined symbol is relocated (verified before choosing the seam).\n\n### Expected shape\n`executor.py` ~790 -> ~655 LOC (~145 lines of headroom, below the 720 near-cap warning band);\nnew `src/rebar/llm/workflow/step_contracts.py` ~190 LOC (over the 100-LOC floor). Moved bodies are\nbyte-identical apart from imports; the four in-`src` lazy import sites are repointed at the new\nmodule, and `executor` keeps re-exporting `contract_for` / `shallow_contract_check` / `StepContract`\nso every existing import (including tests) resolves unchanged.\n\n### Verification\n- `wc -l` on both files; the CI module-size gate with `.github/module-size-limit.txt` untouched.\n- `pytest tests/unit/workflow tests/unit/test_plan_review_workflow_ops_seam.py tests/scripts/test_llm_seam_clean_heldout.py tests/interfaces/store/test_workflow_scripted_steps.py`\n  with NO test file edited except a stale `file:line` prose citation forced by the move.\n- `make lint` (includes `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` -> new=0 increased=0)\n  and `make typecheck`.\n- Serena `find_referencing_symbols` on each moved symbol PLUS a plain `grep` for each name as a\n  string (monkeypatch.setattr / getattr / importlib).\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787187115650819001,"uuid":"86c02b79-7d7d-400e-ab34-e6a04804eb9e"}